  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 230 PM MDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nSynoptically, not much has changed; the ridge that has been in \nplace over the Four Corners region is still likely to stay \nnearly stationary over the next week. By Saturday, guidance is \nstarting to signal that the ridge will begin to de-amplify as a \ntrough moves along the US/Canada border. Throughout the week, \nthere should be a few shortwaves moving through the area. \n\nHigh temperatures for the week look to \"cool\" down to the mid \nto high 90s. Winds look to remain calm to breezy, with minimum \nrelative humidity values staying above 25%. \n\nMoving to precipitation chances for the period: \n\nTuesday's PoP's are currently forecast to be in the 30-60% \nrange, with the highest percentages in eastern Colorado. \nGuidance indicates that the surface low just to the south of the\nCWA will likely remain there throughout the day. With the low \nstationary, winds should mainly be from the east-northeast, \npromoting upslope flow for showers/storms. After looking at \ninstability and shear for severe storm potential, there are \nsigns that if storms were develop, they would mainly stay on the\nmarginal side. For the northwestern portion of the CWA, there \nis a 25-40% chance of exceeding 0.10\" of precipitation from the \nafternoon into early Wednesday morning. \n\nWednesday still looks to have higher precipitation chances of \nthe long-term period as a shortwave propagates through during \nthe afternoon and evening. Guidance indicates a boundary moving \nin from the northwest toward the southeast, though models \ndisagree on its exact location and timing. Currently, both the \nGFS and Canadian models show the boundary; however, the GFS \nbrings it in late afternoon, whereas the Canadian delays it \nuntil early Thursday morning. Other guidance doesn't have the \nboundary. If it does materialize, severe storm potential would \nbe marginal. PoPs are currently forecast to be 40-65% where once\nagain eastern Colorado having the highest percentages. Eastern \nColorado currently has a 25\u201345% chance of exceeding 0.10\" of \nprecipitation. Given this is roughly four days out, it will be \ninteresting to see how guidance trends for the location and \nspeed of the boundary. \n\nFor the rest of the week and weekend, PoPs steadily decrease. \nThursday\u2019s chances are around 20\u201330%, and Friday through Sunday \nstays around 10-20% each day. However, with shortwaves passing \nthrough, any favorable lifting mechanism, like a boundary or \nfront, could nudge these precipitation chances up or down as we \nget closer.",
